  As you so rightly point out, the interface is immaterial.
(And trying to pre-emptively define the question strictly in 
terms of the interface used is a bit...naughty.  Bad DD, no
doughnut.  ;^)

  It's using an external (to the game) means to circumvent an 
intended in-game result.  Much as taking notes when playing a 
game of concentration.  Note that the non-individual item 
"#names" in the game will *also* be erased by a memory loss in 
game, from whatever source.

  So using an external file to record that information does not
give the same *result* after character memory-loss as using
non-individual item "#names".

  The importance of that difference is up to each individual 
player to evaluate.  Just as each individual player must decide
whether save-scumming to circumvent in-game results is 
acceptable.

  Individual item #names are apparently not affected by 
mindflayer brain sucking attacks.  This may be a clue that
this is an acceptable means of obviating the effects of character
memory loss.

  But that's all it can be, a clue.  Just as the argument that
"anything the game lets you do is acceptable" is persuasive.  But
even that argument won't convince everyone.

  The original poster stated the main concern was not having
ascensions tainted in the eyes of others.  If you want other 
players approval, you have to do what they approve of.

  So the only real solution to that problem (that I see) is to 
not let the character suffer from memory-loss in the game.  This
can be done with sufficient care.  (It's just tedious. ;-)

  I'm still happy to discuss the arguments for/against what 
should, or should not, be considered acceptable.  (Which is good,
because I'm doing it. ;^)  I accept that nothing will really be
changed by doing so, however


  And again, I agree to your point that the interface is 
immaterial.  But I also think that discussing the problem 
strictly in terms of interface does not address the heart of the 
issue.



